### What went wrong

The generated command ran as:

```sh
find . -type f ( -name '*.ts' -o ... ) ...
```

Bare `(` is a shell metacharacter, so `/bin/sh` aborted with a syntax error before `find` executed. The backslashes were stripped somewhere in the pipeline that assembled the command.

### Fix

Escape the parens (note `\\(` / `\\)` when the command passes through another shell layer):

```sh
find . -type f \( -name '*.ts' -o -name '*.tsx' -o -name '*.mjs' -o -name '*.js' -o -name '*.cjs' -o -name '*.jsx' -o -name '*.py' -o -name '*.java' -o -name '*.go' -o -name '*.rb' -o -name '*.php' \) \
  -not -path '*/node_modules/*' -not -path '*/.git/*' -not -path '*/.paaw/*' \
  -not -path '*/dist/*' -not -path '*/build/*' -not -path '*/coverage/*' \
  -not -path '*/data/semgrep-rules/*'
```

More robust alternative — since this is a git repo, skip `find` entirely and avoid the escaping hazard:

```sh
git ls-files -- ':(exclude)*.lock' | grep -E '\.(ts|tsx|mjs|js|cjs|jsx|py|java|go|rb|php)$'
```

